---
import PolicyPage from "@/components/layout/PolicyPage.astro";
import { updateManifest } from "@/data/policies";
import { site } from "@/data/site";
---

<PolicyPage
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="Muelle has no accounts, analytics or telemetry. Its optional network requests check for updates and read AI provider usage."
  updated="November 16, 2026"
>
  <p>
    Muelle has no accounts, no analytics and no telemetry. It does not collect personal data and never sends anything
    to the Muelle project. It makes only the two kinds of requests below, and both can be turned off in Settings.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Update check</h2>
  <p>
    When you open Settings, and once a day shortly after you turn on the computer, Muelle asks GitHub
    whether a newer version exists by downloading{" "}
    <code>{updateManifest}</code>. Nothing about you or your computer is sent with it; GitHub, like any website, sees
    your IP address and handles it under the{" "}
    <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub privacy statement</a>.
    When there is one, its signed installer is downloaded from the same GitHub releases page and
    installed: by itself if <b>Install updates automatically</b> is on, otherwise when you choose to.
  </p>
  <p>To turn it off: Settings → System → <b>Check for updates automatically</b> (or only <b>Install updates automatically</b>, to keep the notice in Settings). You can still check by hand.</p>

  <h2>2. AI provider usage</h2>
  <p>
    The menu bar can show available plan limits for Claude Code, Codex,
    OpenCode Go, Gemini CLI, or Antigravity. It checks usage only while a supported client is
    running. Gemini Pro and Flash quotas are shown as separate model families when the provider
    supplies them.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It reads the sign-in that each command-line client already stores on your computer: Claude Code,
      Codex, OpenCode, Gemini CLI or Antigravity. Muelle never decrypts other
      apps' stored secrets or reads their databases, and does not save or change these credentials.
    </li>
    <li>
      With that sign-in it asks the corresponding provider for available limits every five minutes while the indicator is visible, or when you press Refresh:
      Anthropic, OpenAI, OpenCode, or Google. The sign-in only goes to its own provider.
      Antigravity usage is read from its authenticated local service on <code>127.0.0.1</code>.
      Percentages and reset times stay in memory and are shown in the menu bar.
    </li>
    <li>
      For Codex it may instead read the latest limits that Codex itself writes at the end of its session files
      (<code>.codex\sessions</code>), without contacting anyone. It reads only those figures.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each provider handles its requests under its own policy, including{" "}
      <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Anthropic privacy policy</a>,{" "}
      <a href="https://openai.com/policies/privacy-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenAI privacy policy</a>,{" "}
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub privacy statement</a>, and{" "}
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google privacy policy</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>To turn it off: Settings → Menu bar → <b>Show AI provider usage</b>.</p>

  <h2>What stays on your computer</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>%APPDATA%\Muelle\config.json</code>: your settings and the apps you pinned.</li>
    <li><code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Muelle</code>: a cache of app icons and names, so the dock starts fast.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To show what is on screen (open windows, their icons, network and volume status, and whether an app is using the
    microphone or camera, for the menu bar), Muelle asks Windows itself; none of it leaves your computer. Uninstalling removes the cache, and optionally your settings.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    The site is hosted on Vercel and uses no cookies, analytics or trackers. Vercel may keep standard server logs (such
    as IP addresses) under the{" "}
    <a href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vercel privacy policy</a>.
    Downloads come from <a href={site.releases} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Releases</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={site.issues} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open an issue on GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</PolicyPage>
